+# BLOCKED: `002_unique_email.sql` cannot be applied to the production snapshot
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` makes `users.email` `NOT NULL` and
+case-insensitively unique. It applies cleanly to a fresh database and passes
+`test_constraint_on_fresh_db`.
+
+It **cannot** be applied to `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` without failing, and
+`test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` therefore fails. The migration intentionally
+does not touch customer rows, so this cannot be fixed inside the migration.
+
+## Why
+
+The migration must enforce two invariants over the existing rows:
+
+1. `email IS NOT NULL`
+2. `lower(email)` is unique
+
+SQLite validates both against existing data. A `UNIQUE` index is checked for
+every existing row when it is created, and changing a column to `NOT NULL`
+requires rebuilding the table, which copies (and therefore validates) every
+row. The production snapshot violates both invariants:
+
+- **NULL email**: id 17 (`Goran Okafor`) has `email = NULL`.
+- **Case-insensitive duplicate**: ids 1 (`ana.silva@example.com`),
+  4 (`Ana.Silva@example.com`) and 23 (`ana.silva@example.com`) all map to
+  `lower(email) = 'ana.silva@example.com'`.
+
+The task forbids modifying or deleting customer rows ("Support owns that
+data"). Deduplicating ids 1/4/23, inventing an email for id 17, or dropping
+rows would all change customer data, so none of those is an acceptable
+workaround. Weakening the constraint (a partial index, trigger-only checks, or
+leaving `email` nullable) would leave production data violating the invariant
+the migration is supposed to establish, which is also not acceptable.
+
+This is not a limitation of the SQL: no migration can make these existing rows
+satisfy the constraints while leaving them byte-for-byte unchanged.
+
+## What Support needs to do
+
+Before this migration can be applied to production:
+
+1. Decide the real email for id 17 (`Goran Okafor`) and set it.
+2. Resolve ids 1, 4 and 23 (`ana.silva@example.com` /
+   `Ana.Silva@example.com`): determine which row(s) are genuine, then merge,
+   correct or remove the duplicates as Support sees fit.
+
+Once those rows satisfy the invariants, `002_unique_email.sql` will apply to
+the snapshot unchanged and the rows will be preserved by the table rebuild.
+
+## Status
+
+- Fresh database: **done** - `NOT NULL` and case-insensitive uniqueness are
+  enforced.
+- Production snapshot: **blocked** pending the data cleanup above.
+
+## Verification
+
+`python3 -m unittest -v test_migrations`:
+
+```
+test_constraint_on_fresh_db ... ok
+test_applies_to_prod_snapshot ... ERROR
+sqlite3.IntegrityError: NOT NULL constraint failed: users_new.email
+```
+
+The error is expected and is the blocker described above. It is not a defect in
+the migration and must not be "fixed" by pre-cleaning data, weakening the
+constraint, or moving enforcement into triggers: any of those would leave the
+production data violating the invariant this migration is meant to establish.
